\section*{Topic 5: Generators}

Each problem is worth 2 points (one point per printed line).

Generators produce values on demand rather than all at once. This topic
covers the \texttt{yield} syntax, generator comprehensions, lazy
built-ins (\texttt{map}, \texttt{filter}, \texttt{zip},
\texttt{enumerate}, \texttt{itertools}), hand-written iterator classes,
and generator-based implementations of familiar containers.

\subsection*{1. Yield Syntax}

A function that contains \texttt{yield} is a \emph{generator function}.
Calling it does not run the body; it returns a generator object. The
body executes incrementally, yielding one value per \texttt{next()}
call and raising \texttt{StopIteration} when the function returns.

\subsubsection*{1.1 Basics}

Key points:

\begin{itemize}
  \item A generator object can be iterated only once. A second
        \texttt{list()} on the same object sees an empty sequence.
  \item A missing \texttt{yield} makes the function return
        \texttt{None}, so \texttt{list()} on the result raises
        \texttt{TypeError}.
  \item \texttt{return} inside a generator ends it early; subsequent
        \texttt{yield}s are never reached.
  \item \texttt{next()} on an exhausted generator raises
        \texttt{StopIteration}.
\end{itemize}

